<h2>Indian fish meal for aquaculture feed</h2>
<p>Fish meal is one of the less-visible parts of India's seafood export trade — produced from processing by-catch and trimmings at the same coastal plants that handle shrimp and other seafood for export, then steam-dried, pressed and milled into a high-protein feed ingredient. The buyer at the other end is almost always an aquaculture feed mill, not a poultry or cattle feed producer.</p>

<p>We source from coastal processing clusters in Gujarat, Kerala, Tamil Nadu and Andhra Pradesh, with production running through the year as it tracks each region's fishing season rather than a single harvest window.</p>

<h2>Who buys Indian fish meal — and why it matters for your spec</h2>
<p>China takes roughly a third of India's fish meal exports by value, and Vietnam is the second-largest market — both countries have large shrimp-aquaculture sectors, and fish meal is a core ingredient in shrimp starter and grower feeds. Japan, Bangladesh and Saudi Arabia round out the top markets. This buyer profile matters because it shapes what "quality" means in practice: aquaculture feed mills generally care most about protein level, freshness (low TVN, indicating the raw material wasn't degraded before processing) and consistent moisture control — not the broader nutritional-balance concerns a livestock feed formulator might prioritize.</p>
<p>If your use case is livestock or poultry feed rather than aquaculture, tell us — the product can serve that market too, but it's worth confirming your specific requirements since our default production and QC focus follows where the bulk of Indian fish meal actually goes.</p>

<h2>How should I specify fish meal?</h2>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Protein level.</strong> 60% and 65% minimum crude protein are the two standard export grades. Higher-protein feed formulations (shrimp starter feed, for example) typically call for the 65% grade.</li>
  <li><strong>Freshness indicators.</strong> TVN (total volatile nitrogen) is the standard proxy for how fresh the raw material was before processing — ask for a test result if this matters for your formulation.</li>
  <li><strong>Salt and moisture.</strong> Both affect shelf life and handling. Standard export specs cap salt at 3% and moisture at 10%, but confirm your tolerance if your feed mill has tighter requirements.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Pricing and lead time</h2>
<p>Fish meal pricing tracks both the underlying raw-material (by-catch/trimmings) availability and global protein-meal markets more broadly. Send your required protein grade, quantity and destination port and we'll come back with a current FOB/CIF quote.</p>

